WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.889
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We turn then to the annual
reports.

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:12.600
For the remainder of the agenda, we have two
principal items.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.330
One is the annual reports of the committees,
and the other one is the actual recent reports

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.619
of the committees, for recent business.

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:22.680
What I would like to do, with your permission,
is to break this into two things.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.630
First of all, for us the board to approve
the proposed revisions to the terms of reference

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:30.710
that are set out in the covering paper.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.780
I hope we are content to do that.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.890
Secondly, if I could just invite in turn the
respective chairs of the committees to say,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.899
if they wish, anything about a) their annual
report and b) about their routine report.

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:43.899
May I start with you, David?

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:47.289
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think on the routine report,
taken as read.

00:00:47.289 --> 00:00:58.761
On the annual report, the Audit Committee's
focus, obviously alongside the annual report

00:00:58.761 --> 00:01:05.000
and accounts, has really been on risk, governance
and control.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.420
We oversee internal audit.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:14.020
We have had extensive conversations there
on closure of internal audit findings and

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.360
the embedding of those.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.380
I would point the board to the risk and governance
project, which has made material changes and

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:27.720
improvements and simplification in the way
we think about risk and governance, and I

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.270
think that is now in an embedding phase, but
that is the hardest bit.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:41.120
Then the final thing is also, just in the
times we live in, the management of information;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.670
in particular, cyber risk has also been a
focus for the audit committee.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.930
I think as is the norm with the Audit Committee,
all of the above become our core agenda for

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:59.220
the next year, but I shall be delighted to
hand it over to somebody better equipped than

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.119
I am to take it forward.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:05.440
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Well, thank you for that;
there is nobody better equipped, but we do

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.110
hope to be in a position shortly to announce
the appointment of a new non-executive director

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.680
who would also then become chair of Audit
and Risk Assurance Committee.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.620
David, thank you for that.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.440
I will come back to you, if I may, on Commissioning
Committee, but we will allow somebody else

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.500
to make an intervening contribution.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.100
Noel, may I turn to you, please, on specialised
commissioning?

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.300
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:32.840
The work of the Specialised Commissioning
Committee since I took over the chairmanship

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.980
one year ago, and John joined me as Deputy
Chairman, is well set out in the document

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.480
and I really do not want to cover more of
the detail.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:46.480
I know it is of acute interest to many stakeholders
and patients; quite rightly so.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:55.520
Could I just highlight three themes, however,
with more detail in the two papers if required?

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:02.780
Firstly, our job as committee chairs and deputy
chairs was to assist the extraordinary work

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:09.232
of Jonathan Fielden who joined us as the Executive
Director in the transformation of Specialised

00:03:09.232 --> 00:03:10.232
Commissioning.

00:03:10.232 --> 00:03:17.290
Transformation, really, in three senses: one,
its financial sustainability; two, its ability

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:26.630
to deliver better care to more minority groups
and specialist patients; and three, to really

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:34.200
get to grips with what had been some historical
issues, getting back on an even keel.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.350
I would like to just commend the report to
the committee and to our stakeholders, because

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:44.340
it is now getting back on an even keel, in
all three senses.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:51.120
You know we are at the tip of the spear for
many of the financial pressures that come

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.150
through in innovative drugs and devices.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.560
We are managing to contain that.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:05.810
The pressures are immense, and can be felt
in a number of the areas such as Hep C and

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.650
such as the PrEP situation which is described
in these papers.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.180
They are totally normal, but they are extraordinary
pressures.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:18.849
So I commend the work that Jonathan and the
team have done to get a grip on the managed

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:24.880
access for the Cancer Drugs Fund, as well
as in the new area of innovative drugs and

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:25.880
devices.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:32.590
We have also attacked the historical issue
of reconfiguration, which has bothered the

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:40.100
topology of our delivery of specialised services
for a very long time, particularly with congenital

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:45.320
heart disease which has come forward recently,
and has in a sense tried to address what has

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.230
been an unaddressed problem for a very long
time.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:57.720
Thirdly, in the overall delivery of specialist
services through variation, which has been

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:05.600
partly caused by the lack of policy in a number
of areas and a lack of address, really, of

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.030
variation.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:13.419
So in general, I am extraordinarily content
with where we have got to.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:19.330
There is a huge amount of work still to do,
but I do commend this annual report to all

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:24.590
of our patients and stakeholders who have
an interest in this area, because a huge amount

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.460
of progress has been made; a huge amount of
progress still to make, but we have a grip

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:30.460
on it.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.000
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Noel.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.400
I do not think anyone on the board would underestimate
what has actually happened in the last year

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.970
in specialised commissioning.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.940
So particular thanks to you and to Jonathan
Fielden.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.310
Did you wish to make any comment on the current
report to the board?

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.290
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: No, it is swept up in my general
themes.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:48.650
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:50.470
Thank you very much.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.290
Any questions to Noel?

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:53.290
No

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.230
Moira, may we then have a word about the Investment
Committee?

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.270
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Nothing to add to either the
annual report or the report from the last

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.270
meeting.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.979
Just to say that we look forward to welcoming
Wendy to the Investment Committee since we

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:09.430
lost Noel to greater things earlier this year.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:13.020
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Congratulations, Wendy.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.860
Now, may we then move back to you, please,
David, for Commissioning Committee.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:18.090
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:23.340
Again, I will take the most recent report
as read.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:30.950
In terms of the annual report, I think I would
highlight that the Committee over the last

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:35.710
12 months has carried quite a heavy burden.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:43.780
In terms of the core things that we have focused
on, firstly the aggregate commissioning strategy

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:51.900
and capabilities required shows that we have
a roadmap, as we start to think about how

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.320
the changes that we are all embarked on will
impact commissioning.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:02.639
There had been some comment that commissioning
will die as a result of these changes: accountable

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:03.970
care organisations, MCPs, new models of care.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:10.430
That is clearly not the case; it just changes,
so we need to be ahead of that change.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.750
The second thing then is the big changes themselves.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:20.550
So, the GP Forward View, new models of care,
devolution have all been extensive pieces

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.360
of work that we have spent time looking at.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.639
I think the third thing I would highlight
is the allocations process.

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:37.080
There was some pretty heavy lifting done with
Paul and the team to provide the appropriate

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.800
oversight and governance on allocations.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.630
I think we provided a roadmap there, again.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:47.210
All of this is about providing a longer-term
roadmap for people, so there is more clarity

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.210
and certainty.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:52.550
That was true in the allocations process,
with again some quite big changes there that

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:54.540
were introduced this year.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:08:00.130
Then finally, not to be underestimated: the
CCG assurance process, where we have been

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:09.150
through significant change, and just the day-to-day
financial management of trying to, as Simon

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:26.990
highlighted, successfully land a £101 billion
process last year, a £106.5 billion 

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:29.520
process and the management of that.

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.630
Going forward, three big themes.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:36.870
The transformation programmes, the design
and delivery of those.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:38.789
This is very much a year of delivery.

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:43.709
Therefore, our focus is absolutely on whether
we are ready to deliver, is the quality right

00:08:43.709 --> 00:08:45.309
and so on.

00:08:45.309 --> 00:08:55.129
Secondly, it is performance across the board
against the new CCG assurance domains, and

00:08:55.129 --> 00:08:57.199
included in that, the money.

00:08:57.199 --> 00:09:05.660
Then thirdly, the oversight of the commissioning
system as it changes and develops, which I

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:14.240
think will be the very essence of things like
devolution, new models of care and the things

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.640
we have talked about today.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:24.740
GP Forward View will require considerable
oversight and governance for us.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:28.459
Last year was busy; I suspect this year will
be even busier.

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:31.459
The great news, I think, is last year was
about design.

00:09:31.459 --> 00:09:35.929
This year is much more about delivery, and
that is where we need to spend our time.

00:09:35.929 --> 00:09:38.589
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, David.

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:42.559
I think it is just for me to say, first of
all, that every member of the board serves

00:09:42.559 --> 00:09:47.899
on committees, and this is where so much of
the board's work is done, and where the board

00:09:47.899 --> 00:09:55.899
gets assurance as to the detail of our activities
so that the board can concentrate on the broad

00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:56.899
strategic issues.

00:09:56.899 --> 00:10:00.629
Secondly, it only gets done in committees
because we have got chairmen who are putting

00:10:00.629 --> 00:10:02.540
in a great deal of time and effort.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:07.790
We ought to, I think, just reflect on that
and thank them for their work over the past

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:08.790
year.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.929
The submission of annual reports to the board
is a very good way of taking stock of what

00:10:11.929 --> 00:10:15.259
we have achieved and where we have got to
go to.

00:10:15.259 --> 00:10:18.990
We all know that next year will always be
worse than last year; that is the one reassuring

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:23.829
message that comes repeatedly to the board.

00:10:23.829 --> 00:10:26.050
On that note, any other business?

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.040
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Can I challenge that?

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:35.680
Next year will not be worse than last year;
it will be better, but maybe different.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:41.889
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So next year will be brimming
with opportunities, and we look forward to

00:10:41.889 --> 00:10:43.379
it enormously.

00:10:43.379 --> 00:10:45.769
So may we then turn to any other business?

00:10:45.769 --> 00:10:48.160
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I have only two items.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:53.209
One is to remind the board that our AGM is
on 25th October, and we will bring to the

00:10:53.209 --> 00:10:55.050
AGM our annual report and accounts.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.740
Secondly, Jane, you had an issue that you
wished to raise?

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.050
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Yes, thank you, Chair.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:07.309
I just wanted to use this opportunity to make
a public tribute to Dr Kate Granger, who died

00:11:07.309 --> 00:11:09.139
last Saturday.

00:11:09.139 --> 00:11:15.089
Kate is - was - very well known to people
across the NHS and care system, had a significant

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:21.550
impact on services to patients and created
the #hellomynameis campaign.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:27.389
She also did an awful lot to break down the
barriers about talking openly about living

00:11:27.389 --> 00:11:35.850
with a terminal illness and dying, and her
contribution and her support and input into

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:38.309
care was just absolutely amazing and inspirational.

00:11:38.309 --> 00:11:43.660
So she will be very sadly missed, but I think
her contribution will continue to grow, and

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.759
I think the #hellomynameis campaign will continue.

00:11:46.759 --> 00:11:51.899
I just wanted to use this opportunity on behalf
of NHS England and staff across the NHS and

00:11:51.899 --> 00:11:55.730
care system to pay tribute to her, and give
our very best wishes and condolences to her

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:56.730
husband Chris.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.699
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Jane.

00:11:58.699 --> 00:12:00.319
Thank you very much.

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:04.269
Subject to our normal resolution about private
business, I declare the meeting closed.

00:12:04.269 --> 00:12:04.609
Thank you.

